<article class="env-lemma" id="04H7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04H7">Lemma <span data-tag="04H7">18.40.5</span>.</a> Being a locally ringed site is an intrinsic property. More precisely, </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
if $f : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}') \to \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C})$ is a morphism of topoi and $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ is a locally ringed site, then $(\mathcal{C}', f^{-1}\mathcal{O})$ is a locally ringed site, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $(f, f^\sharp ) : (\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}'), \mathcal{O}') \to (\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}), \mathcal{O})$ is an equivalence of ringed topoi, then $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ is locally ringed if and only if $(\mathcal{C}', \mathcal{O}')$ is locally ringed. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It is clear that (2) follows from (1). To prove (1) note that as $f^{-1}$ is exact we have $f^{-1}* = *$, $f^{-1}\emptyset ^\#  = \emptyset ^\# $, and $f^{-1}$ commutes with products, equalizers and transforms isomorphisms and surjections into isomorphisms and surjections. Thus $f^{-1}$ transforms the isomorphism (<a href="/tag/05D7" data-tag="05D7">18.40.2.1</a>) into its analogue for $f^{-1}\mathcal{O}$ and transforms the surjection of Lemma <a href="/tag/04ES" data-tag="04ES">18.40.1</a> part (3) into the corresponding surjection for $f^{-1}\mathcal{O}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>